// Lesson 3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy
// Problem breakdown: Spawning, aiming, moving, and cleaning up projectiles.
// Reading Unity functions (Instantiate vs Destroy, Translate vs Rigidbody velocity).
// Building Projectile.cs from scratch and writing AttackPlayer in TurretController.cs.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.2 — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret knows when you are near; now it needs teeth. Spawning and firing a projectile
    looks complex, but it breaks down into four smaller sub-problems:
    <strong>spawn a prefab → point it at the player → move it through space → destroy it when done</strong>.
    Today, you read Unity's projectile functions and build <code>Projectile.cs</code> from scratch.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Firing a projectile requires coordinating several distinct Unity functions.
        Read how the engine handles each step:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Sub-Problem</th><th>Unity Function</th><th>Documentation &amp; Purpose</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Spawning</strong></td>
            <td><code>Instantiate(prefab, pos, rot)</code></td>
            <td>Creates a brand-new clone of a prefab at runtime and places it into the scene hierarchy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Aiming</strong></td>
            <td><code>(target - origin).normalized</code></td>
            <td>Vector subtraction produces a vector pointing toward the target. <code>.normalized</code> reduces its length to 1.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Moving</strong></td>
            <td><code>transform.Translate</code> or <code>Rigidbody2D</code></td>
            <td>Moves the bullet every frame along its forward direction.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>4. Cleanup</strong></td>
            <td><code>Destroy(gameObject, delay)</code></td>
            <td>Destroys the projectile after a time delay (seconds) to prevent off-screen bullets from clogging memory.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Multiple Ways to Move Projectiles</h3>
        <p>Compare these two ways to move a projectile across the screen:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Method 1 (Translate):</strong> <code>transform.Translate(Vector3.right * speed * Time.deltaTime);</code></li>
            <li><strong>Method 2 (Physics Velocity):</strong> <code>GetComponent&lt;Rigidbody2D&gt;().linearVelocity = direction * speed;</code></li>
        </ul>
        <ol>
            <li>If you want bullets that bounce off stone walls like in a pinball or billiards game, which method (Method 1 or Method 2) is significantly easier, and why?</li>
            <li>If your projectiles are laser beams, energy arrows, or magic missiles that fly straight through obstacles, why is Method 1 clean and lightweight?</li>
            <li>Why is <code>Destroy(gameObject, 5f)</code> called an essential <strong>garbage collection</strong> pattern? What would happen to your game's framerate if a player dodged 1,000 bullets and they all kept flying into deep space forever?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Notice how each mathematical, rotational, and spawning function works in isolation across completely different game systems:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — Computing Direction and Unit Length on an Airplane Navigation System:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Vector subtraction (destination - current) gives direction; .normalized scales length to 1.0
Vector3 headingDirection = (waypointPosition - transform.position).normalized;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — Converting 2D Direction to Angle on a Weather Vane:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Atan2 calculates the angle in radians from X/Y coordinates; Rad2Deg converts to 0-360 degrees
float windAngle = Mathf.Atan2(windVector.y, windVector.x) * Mathf.Rad2Deg;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Applying 2D Rotation with Quaternion.AngleAxis:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// In 2D games, rotation occurs around the Z axis (Vector3.forward, pointing out of the screen)
transform.rotation = Quaternion.AngleAxis(windAngle, Vector3.forward);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — Spawning and Initializing on a Magic Fountain:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// 1. Stamp clone into world
GameObject orb = Instantiate(orbPrefab, fountainNozzle.position, Quaternion.identity);

// 2. Access component on the fresh clone and pass setup data
orb.GetComponent&lt;MagicOrb&gt;().InitializeOrb(targetCoords);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — Timed Cleanup with Destroy on a Floating Soap Bubble:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Schedules this GameObject to be automatically destroyed 4 seconds after appearing
Destroy(gameObject, 4.0f);</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In Example 1, what happens if you forget <code>.normalized</code>? If the target is 20 units away, how does that affect any math multiplied by that direction?</li>
            <li>In Example 3, why is <code>Vector3.forward</code> (0, 0, 1) used as the axis of rotation for a 2D sprite?</li>
            <li>In Example 5, why is scheduling destruction with a timer better than never destroying dodged projectiles?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>Projectile.cs</code>:</strong>
        <p><strong>Variables &amp; Tuning Knobs:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Where in <code>Assets/Scripts/</code> should you create this script, and what must the class name match?</li>
            <li>What serialized float knobs should designers be able to adjust in the Inspector for bullet speed and maximum time-to-live? What defaults make sense?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Aiming &amp; Initialization:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>When a projectile is spawned, it needs to know where the target was standing. What public method signature can receive that target coordinate?</li>
            <li>How do you calculate a direction vector from where the bullet spawned to where the target is? Why must this vector be normalized?</li>
            <li>What trigonometric function calculates the angle in radians from X and Y components? How do you convert radians into degrees?</li>
            <li>Which 3D axis represents the pivot axis for 2D rotation? What Quaternion method builds a rotation from an angle and an axis?</li>
            <li>How do you tell Unity to automatically clean up this bullet after its lifetime expires, even if it never hits a target?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Per-Frame Flight in Update:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Since the projectile was rotated to face the target upon spawn, which local direction represents "forward" in Unity 2D sprite geometry?</li>
            <li>What function moves the transform each frame? How do you ensure flight speed is frame-rate independent?</li>
            <li>Which prefab in <code>Assets/Prefabs/</code> needs this script attached?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>TurretController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <p><strong>Inspector Slots:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What serialized fields are needed so designers can drag in the projectile prefab and assign the physical spawn nozzle?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Attack Method:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>In your firing function, what guard check ensures the prefab and spawn point slots are not left unassigned?</li>
            <li>What method stamps a new clone of the projectile prefab into the scene? Where should it be placed, and what rotation should it start with?</li>
            <li>How do you grab the <code>Projectile</code> script on that newly created clone? What method do you call on it to aim it at the player's position?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Triggering the Attack:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Inside your range check in <code>Update()</code>, where should this attack method be invoked?</li>
            <li>What slots need to be connected in the Turret's Inspector before testing in Play mode?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and press Play. Walk near the turret.
        A projectile spawns from the muzzle, aims at your position, and shoots across the screen!
        (Notice that it fires 60 bullets every second — in lesson 3.3 we fix the cooldown!)
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Projectile Errors</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you leave the <strong>Projectile Prefab</strong> slot unassigned in the Inspector? What error does Unity throw when you step in range?</li>
            <li>In <code>Projectile.cs</code>, what happens if you change <code>Vector3.right</code> to <code>Vector3.up</code> in <code>Update()</code>? Which direction do bullets fly relative to their aim?</li>
            <li>In <code>Projectile.cs</code>, comment out the <code>Destroy(gameObject, lifetime)</code> line. Fire several bullets and watch the Hierarchy in play mode. Do the spawned instances ever leave the scene?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Bullet Tuning</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In the <code>Turret_Projectile</code> prefab, tune <code>speed</code> between <code>3</code> (slow dodgeable dodgeball) and <code>15</code> (bullet-hell sniper). How does projectile speed alter the player's emotional reaction to the turret?</li>
            <li>Why does <code>Initialize</code> calculate the direction once, instead of continuously updating direction in <code>Update()</code>? What kind of weapon would you get if <code>direction</code> re-aimed toward the player every frame?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Break down projectile firing into instantiation, aiming, translation, and destruction</li>
            <li>Explain the role of <code>Instantiate()</code> in stamping runtime prefab clones</li>
            <li>Explain why vectors are normalized before calculating angle and movement</li>
            <li>Create <code>Projectile.cs</code> and wire the turret's projectile spawn slots</li>
            <li>Commit your projectile system to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

